# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hume-induction.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: hume-induction.logic.r3 has "no_contradiction"; hume-induction.logic.r3.2 and hume-induction.logic.r3.3 have "no_contradiction → ¬demo_arg"; they differ when only "there is a demonstrative argument (relations of ideas) that the future will be conformable to the past" and "it implies no contradiction that the course of nature may change" are true, which the first allows
hume-induction.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps hume-induction.logic.r3.2 and hume-induction.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "there is a demonstrative argument (relations of ideas) that the future will be conformable to the past" and "it implies no contradiction that the course of nature may change" are true. Is S2, "That there are no demonstrative arguments in the case seems evident; since it implies no contradiction that the course of nature may change, and that an object, seemingly like those which we have experienced, may be attended with different or contrary effects.", true then?
hume-induction.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: hume-induction.logic.r3 has "conceivable → no_contradiction ∧ ¬demo_arg"; hume-induction.logic.r3.2 and hume-induction.logic.r3.3 have "conceivable → no_contradiction"; they differ when only "there is a demonstrative argument (relations of ideas) that the future will be conformable to the past", "it implies no contradiction that the course of nature may change" and "that the course of nature may change, and like objects have contrary effects, can be clearly and distinctly conceived" are true, which the second allows
hume-induction.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps hume-induction.logic.r3, to: Suppose "there is a demonstrative argument (relations of ideas) that the future will be conformable to the past", "it implies no contradiction that the course of nature may change" and "that the course of nature may change, and like objects have contrary effects, can be clearly and distinctly conceived" are true. Is S5, "Now whatever is intelligible, and can be distinctly conceived, implies no contradiction, and can never be proved false by any demonstrative argument or abstract reasoning à priori.", true then?
hume-induction.md:3: argue/readings: S9 is read 2 ways: hume-induction.logic.r3 has "presuppose_uniformity → ¬prob_arg"; hume-induction.logic.r3.2 and hume-induction.logic.r3.3 have "causal_founded ∧ from_experience ∧ presuppose_uniformity → ¬prob_arg"; they differ when only "there is a probable argument (matter of fact and existence) that the future will be conformable to the past, without going in a circle", "all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect" and "all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past" are true, which the second allows
hume-induction.md:3: argue/answered: S9: answered no, which keeps hume-induction.logic.r3, to: Suppose "there is a probable argument (matter of fact and existence) that the future will be conformable to the past, without going in a circle", "all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect" and "all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past" are true and "our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience" is false. Is S9, "To endeavour, therefore, the proof of this last supposition by probable arguments, or arguments regarding existence, must be evidently going in a circle, and taking that for granted, which is the very point in question.", true then?
hume-induction.md:1: argue/unfaithful: hume-induction.logic.r3 P1 (S1): the sentence has no word of "there are arguments that engage us to trust past experience and make it the standard of our future judgement": is it the sentence P1 came from?
hume-induction.md:1: argue/negation: hume-induction.logic.r3 P3 (S3): the sentence says "not", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "that the course of nature may change, and like objects have contrary effects, can be clearly and distinctly conceived"
hume-induction.md:1: argue/unfaithful: hume-induction.logic.r3.2 P1 (S1): the sentence has no word of "there are arguments that engage us to trust past experience and make it the standard of our future judgement": is it the sentence P1 came from?
